3.5.2. Infrastructure Data Collection

The data flow begins with infrastructure devices within the Mini Data Center. Servers and their monitored resources generate operational metrics during system operation.

The main metrics collected by the system include:

CPU usage
RAM usage
Disk usage
Network status

The Collector Agent, implemented using Python, is responsible for collecting these metrics from the monitored infrastructure.

The Collector periodically obtains the current state of the devices and prepares the collected information as telemetry data.

The basic flow is:

Server / Container
       ↓
System Metrics
       ↓
Collector Agent

The Collector therefore acts as the connection between the physical infrastructure and the AR-IMMS Backend.

3.5.3. Telemetry Transmission

After collecting infrastructure metrics, the Collector sends the telemetry data to the Backend.

The telemetry data contains information associated with the monitored device and its corresponding measurements. A telemetry record can conceptually contain:

Device ID
Timestamp
CPU Usage
RAM Usage
Disk Usage
Network Status

The data flow is:

Collector Agent
       ↓
Telemetry Data
       ↓
Backend

The Backend receives the telemetry data and processes it for storage, monitoring, and anomaly detection.

3.5.4. Telemetry Processing

The Backend is responsible for receiving and processing telemetry data from the Collector.

After receiving telemetry, the Backend performs the necessary processing before storing the information in the Database and providing it to the monitoring applications.

The main processing flow is:

Telemetry
    ↓
Backend
    ↓
Validation / Processing
    ↓
 ┌───────────────┬────────────────┐
 ↓               ↓                ↓
Database     Alert Engine     Real-time Data

This architecture allows the same telemetry information to support both historical data storage and real-time infrastructure monitoring.

3.5.5. Data Storage

Processed telemetry and other system information are stored in the PostgreSQL Database.

The Database stores information required for the operation of AR-IMMS, including:

User accounts and roles
Infrastructure information
Server and Container information
Telemetry data
Alerts
Incidents
Tickets
Maintenance records
Digital Twin-related information
Audit records

The data flow is:

Backend
   ↓
PostgreSQL Database

The Database provides persistent storage, allowing the system to retrieve previously collected information and maintain the history of infrastructure events.

3.5.6. Anomaly Detection and Alert Generation

The Backend provides an Alert Engine for detecting abnormal infrastructure conditions based on predefined thresholds or conditions.

The process can be represented as:

Telemetry
    ↓
Alert Engine
    ↓
Check predefined conditions
    ↓
 ┌───────────────┐
 │ Abnormal?     │
 └───────┬───────┘
         │
    ┌────┴────┐
    │         │
   No        Yes
    │         │
    ↓         ↓
 Continue    Alert
 monitoring   ↓
           Database

When the telemetry data does not satisfy the defined conditions, the system continues normal monitoring.

When an abnormal condition is detected, the system generates an Alert and stores the corresponding information.

The Alert lifecycle is managed according to the system requirements:

OPEN
  ↓
ACKNOWLEDGED
  ↓
RESOLVED
  ↓
CLOSED
3.5.7. Incident and Ticket Data Flow

When an Alert requires formal investigation or handling, it can be associated with an Incident.

The Incident can then lead to the creation of a Ticket for tracking the required maintenance or resolution work.

The workflow is:

Alert
  ↓
Incident
  ↓
Ticket
  ↓
Technician
  ↓
Maintenance
  ↓
Resolution

The Ticket lifecycle is:

OPEN
  ↓
IN_PROGRESS
  ↓
RESOLVED
  ↓
CLOSED

This flow connects infrastructure monitoring with the maintenance process, allowing detected problems to be tracked until resolution.

3.5.8. Real-time Data Distribution

The Backend uses Socket.IO to distribute real-time updates to the Web Dashboard and Mobile AR application.

After telemetry is processed, the Backend can transmit updated infrastructure information to connected clients.

The flow is:

Backend
   ↓
Socket.IO
  ┌┴─────────────┐
  ↓              ↓
Web Dashboard   Mobile AR

The Web Dashboard can use these updates to display current infrastructure status and telemetry.

The Mobile AR application can use the updates to display relevant device information when a technician identifies a physical device.

This real-time communication reduces the need for users to manually refresh the application to obtain updated monitoring information.

3.5.9. Web Dashboard Data Flow

The Web Dashboard is developed using Next.js and provides a centralized interface for authorized users.

The main information displayed by the Web Dashboard includes:

Infrastructure status
Telemetry
Alerts
Incidents
Tickets
Maintenance status

The communication flow is:

Backend
   │
   ├── REST API ─────→ Web Dashboard
   │
   └── Socket.IO ────→ Web Dashboard

REST API is used for requesting and managing system resources, while Socket.IO is used for real-time updates.

3.5.10. Mobile AR Data Flow

The Mobile AR application is developed using React Native and supports technicians during on-site inspection and maintenance.

The application uses QR or ArUco markers to identify physical devices.

The basic process is:

Physical Device
      ↓
QR / ArUco Marker
      ↓
Mobile AR
      ↓
Identify Device
      ↓
Request Device Information
      ↓
Backend
      ↓
Telemetry / Alert / Ticket
      ↓
AR Information Overlay

After identifying a device, the Mobile AR application can obtain relevant information from the Backend and display it through the AR interface.

This allows digital infrastructure information to be connected to the corresponding physical device.

3.5.11. Summary of Data Flow

The complete data flow of AR-IMMS can therefore be summarized as:

Mini Data Center
       ↓
Collector Agent
       ↓
Telemetry
       ↓
Backend
   ┌───┼───────────────┐
   ↓   ↓               ↓
Database Alert Engine  Digital Twin
       ↓
Alert → Incident → Ticket
       ↓
   Socket.IO
      ┌┴───────┐
      ↓        ↓
     Web     Mobile AR

The DFD demonstrates how AR-IMMS integrates infrastructure data collection, backend processing, persistent storage, anomaly detection, operational management, and real-time visualization into a single monitoring and maintenance workflow.

3.6. KIẾN TRÚC TRIỂN KHAI (DEPLOYMENT ARCHITECTURE)
Hệ thống AR-IMMS được triển khai theo mô hình phân tán, kết nối trực tiếp từ tầng thu thập dữ liệu tại Data Center lên hệ thống quản lý trung tâm.
3.6.1. Thu thập dữ liệu tại biên (Mini Data Center & Collector)
Mini Data Center / Server: Là các máy chủ hoặc thiết bị vật lý nằm tại trung tâm dữ liệu cần được giám sát.
Collector Agent (Python): Được cài đặt trực tiếp trên các máy chủ mục tiêu. Agent hoạt động như một tiến trình ngầm, liên tục đo lường thông số phần cứng (CPU, RAM, Disk, Network) và gửi trực tiếp về Backend.
3.6.2. Xử lý trung tâm (Backend & Database)
Backend (NestJS): Đóng vai trò là bộ não của hệ thống, xử lý logic nghiệp vụ, quản lý cảnh báo (Alert Engine), và phân quyền xác thực. Backend cung cấp hai luồng giao tiếp chính: REST API cho các tác vụ quản trị và Socket.IO cho dữ liệu thời gian thực.
Database (SQL Server): Lưu trữ tập trung toàn bộ dữ liệu bền vững bao gồm cấu trúc hạ tầng, thông tin người dùng, nhật ký (Audit) và các dữ liệu Telemetry đã qua xử lý.
3.6.3. Tầng giao diện người dùng (Web & Mobile AR)
Web Portal (Next.js): Ứng dụng web phục vụ Administrator và Operator để giám sát toàn cảnh, quản lý sự cố và cấu hình hạ tầng.
Mobile AR (React Native): Ứng dụng di động dành cho Technician, tích hợp tính năng quét QR/ArUco Marker tại Data Center để nhận diện thiết bị và hiển thị thông tin bằng công nghệ AR.
